# TASK 10 – Compare Expectations with Execution

## 1. Introduction

In this task, I compare the results obtained using different data types for calculating the metro's daily fare collection.

I have used `double` to store the fare in rupees and `long long` to store it in paise. I also checked whether `int` can store the total collection when the number of journeys increases.

## 2. Daily Collection Calculation

For this experiment, I have taken ₹36 as the fare for each journey.

The total collection is calculated using:

**Total Collection = Number of Journeys × Fare**

The calculations are:

- 20,000 journeys = ₹7,20,000
- 5,00,000 journeys = ₹1,80,00,000
- 30,00,000 journeys = ₹10,80,00,000

## 3. C++ Program

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <iomanip>
#include <climits>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    long long journeys[] = {20000, 500000, 3000000};

    double fare = 36.0;
    long long farePaise = 3600;

    cout << fixed << setprecision(2);

    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)
    {
        double doubleTotal = journeys[i] * fare;

        long long paiseTotal = journeys[i] * farePaise;

        cout << "Journeys: " << journeys[i] << endl;

        cout << "Double total: Rs "
             << doubleTotal << endl;

        cout << "Paise total: "
             << paiseTotal << " paise" << endl;

        cout << "Difference: Rs "
             << doubleTotal - paiseTotal / 100.0 << endl;

        if (paiseTotal > INT_MAX)
            cout << "int is not enough.\n";
        else
            cout << "int can store this total.\n";

        cout << endl;
    }

    // Precision test
    double total = 0.0;
    long long totalPaise = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
    {
        total = total + 0.1;
        totalPaise = totalPaise + 10;
    }

    cout << "PRECISION TEST\n";
    cout << setprecision(17);

    cout << "Double total: " << total << endl;
    cout << "Integer paise total: "
         << totalPaise << " paise" << endl;

    cout << "Maximum int value: " << INT_MAX << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

## 4. Expected Results

| Number of journeys | Double total | Integer paise total | Difference |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 20,000 | ₹7,20,000 | 72,000,000 paise | ₹0.00 |
| 5,00,000 | ₹1,80,00,000 | 1,800,000,000 paise | ₹0.00 |
| 30,00,000 | ₹10,80,00,000 | 10,800,000,000 paise | ₹0.00 |

Both methods give the same result for these calculations because the fare used is ₹36, which can be represented exactly in this calculation.

